<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Hardened-runtime entitlements for the notarized Cairn build.

    Deliberately minimal. Cairn is local-first (see docs/PRIVACY.md):
    the ONLY outbound network it makes is (a) fetching subscribed ICS
    calendar URLs and (b) the opt-in update check. macOS has no
    finer-grained outbound entitlement than network.client, so that one
    key covers both. There is no network.server entitlement — Cairn
    never listens on a network socket (its IPC is a Unix domain socket /
    named pipe, which needs no entitlement).

    NOT granted here, on purpose:
      - network.server: Cairn never accepts inbound network connections.
      - files.user-selected / downloads: CSV export uses the standard
        save panel, which is powerbox-mediated and needs no entitlement.
      - Accessibility: reading the frontmost window TITLE uses the
        Accessibility API, but that is a runtime TCC permission the user
        grants in System Settings - it is NOT an entitlement and signing
        is what lets the app appear in that list at all.

    If the WKWebView fails to load under hardened runtime on an older
    macOS, add `com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit` (true) - but verify it
    is actually required before weakening the runtime.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    App Group shared with the Safari Web Extension (#250).

    The extension's handler runs under the App Sandbox, which refuses
    connect(2) to a Unix socket outside its container - proven in
    browser-extension/safari/spike/SPIKE.md, where the only variable
    between the working and the EPERM case was the sandbox entitlement.
    The group container is the one filesystem location both the
    non-sandboxed main app and the sandboxed extension can reach, so the
    browser IPC socket binds there on macOS
    (~/Library/Group Containers/<group>/ipc/sock).

    This does NOT sandbox Cairn. The main app stays a non-sandboxed
    Developer ID build; app-groups and app-sandbox are separate
    entitlements, and only the extension gets the latter. 1Password ships
    exactly this combination (group entitlement, no sandbox).

    The ZA246B9H75. prefix is the Apple Team ID and is required: macOS
    only grants this entitlement for a group owned by the signing team.

    Declaring it here is NOT enough on its own. On a Developer ID build
    macOS honours a restricted entitlement only when the bundle embeds a
    provisioning profile authorizing it, so the release job embeds one
    from the APPLE_PROVISIONING_PROFILE secret and then FAILS if it
    didn't land - an unauthorized entitlement reports no error anywhere,
    it just silently costs us the group container. Creating that profile
    needs the App Groups capability enabled on the App ID in the
    developer account. See "macOS App Group provisioning" in RELEASING.md.

    Keep in lockstep with APP_GROUP_ID in
    src-tauri/src/plugins/browser/mod.rs, MACOS_APP_GROUP_ID in
    browser-extension/native-host/src/main.rs, and `group` in
    browser-extension/safari/build-wrapper.sh.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
  <array>
    <string>ZA246B9H75.group.io.drmowinckels.cairn</string>
  </array>
</dict>
</plist>
